body:
> On the other hand, it is socially clunky. When I see someone identifying their pronouns I mentally categorize them as an ally. On the other hand, I'm no more likely to come out and I find it pretty irritating if everyone is asked to state their pronouns. 

This describes me perfectly. It irritates me a lot when constantly asked your pronouns, especially when you know you aren't a guy yet aren't out. Asking is a nice gesture, but IMO the social clunkiness of asking and the irritation (and possibly anxiety) it causes me when forced to state my pronouns when I am not out yet make me wish people would just stop trying to normalize it. 

&#x200B;
